    "sVXzeOo4": {
      "why": "Overview: \"All PhD students must complete the required coursework for a first, second, and third field ... All courses that fulfill Program and Field requirements must be taken for a letter grade.\" The Bulletin data asked for one field's courses. Now three choices: a first field (core for five units each plus the first-field electives: two for American, Comparative and Formal Theory, one for International Relations, Quantitative Methodology and Political Theory), a second field (core plus one elective for American, Comparative and Formal Theory, none for the others), and a third field (\"two courses for at least three units\": two core or elective classes for American, Comparative and International Relations, two Political Theory core classes, POLISCI 450C and 450D, POLISCI 356A and 356B, or a self-designed field). Electives \"must be taken for at least three units\"; QM's \"450D may be taken for three units\". POLISCI 450A and 450B are also the program's Quantitative Methods requirement, so in the QM core they also count there. That the three fields differ, and that Formal Theory and Quantitative Methodology are not both chosen, is checked by hand (ov-fields-distinct). Grading: \"all students must earn a minimum grade of A- for courses taken to fulfill first and second field requirements.\" Research Design note: \"Students with Comparative Politics as a first field must take 440C\", so the Comparative first field asks for POLISCI 440C, which also counts as the research design course.",
